::Q1::What does "raw data" mean in the context of a data lake?
{
~Data that has been deleted from the source system
~Data that has already been aggregated into final reports
=Data that has been collected but may not yet have been cleaned or transformed for a specific analytical use
~Data that can only be stored after being converted to relational tables
}


::Q2::What does schema-on-write generally imply? 
{
=Data is structured according to a defined schema before or as it is written to the analytical store
~Data is never given any structure
~A schema is created only after every query finishes
~Only unstructured files can be loaded
}

::Q3::What does schema-on-read generally imply? 
{
~Every source must match a warehouse table before storage
=Structure is applied when the data is read or analyzed rather than requiring one fixed structure at ingestion
~All data must be transformed into a single relational schema immediately
~No structure can ever be applied to the data
}

::Q4::Why might an organization use both a data lake and a data warehouse? 
{
~The two systems perform exactly the same role
=The lake can retain raw varied data while the warehouse provides structured data for reliable analysis
~A warehouse is needed only to store operating-system files
~Using both guarantees that no transformation is ever required
}

::Q5::Which statement best captures the relationship between a lake and a warehouse? 
{
=They serve different but complementary data-management and analytical purposes
~They are identical systems with different file extensions
~A data lake is simply another name for an operational database
~A data warehouse is only a backup copy of a data lake
}